# Part One — The Schools of Feminism

# Chapter 1 · What Is Feminism?

> Before you can compare the schools of feminism, you need to know what they share. This chapter defines feminism, traces the word, clears away the common myths, and shows that feminism has South Asian and Muslim roots as well as Western ones. It answers the first half of the 2016 question: "What is feminism?"

## 1.1  Feminism in one sentence

In the simplest words, **feminism** is the belief that women and men should have **equal rights, equal dignity and equal chances in life**, together with the **action** taken to make that belief real.

So feminism has two parts:

1. **An idea (a theory).** Women as a group are disadvantaged — in law, work, family, politics and culture — and this disadvantage is **made by society**, not fixed by nature. (Topic 1 taught you the sex/gender distinction on which this rests.)
2. **A practice (a movement).** People organise to change that situation — through writing, campaigns, protests, lawsuits, votes, laws and new ways of living.

That is why the syllabus head is called **"Feminist Theories and Practice"**. A school of feminism is always both: a way of *explaining* women's position and a plan for *changing* it.

::: define Feminism | the South Asian definition to quote in Pakistan
"An awareness of women's oppression and exploitation in society, at work and within the family, and conscious action by women and men to change this situation."
-> Kamla Bhasin and Nighat Said Khan, *Some Questions on Feminism and its Relevance in South Asia* (==1986==). Nighat Said Khan is a Pakistani feminist scholar. Note two features: it names **three sites** (society, work, family) and it includes **men**.
:::

::: define Feminism | the shortest definition
"Simply put, feminism is a movement to end sexism, sexist exploitation, and oppression."
-> bell hooks, *Feminism Is for Everybody* (==2000==). hooks spelt her name in small letters. The definition targets **sexism**, not men — so men can be feminists too, and women can be sexist.
:::

## 1.2  Three older definitions worth knowing

- **Rebecca West** (British writer, ==1913==) joked: "I myself have never been able to find out precisely what feminism is: I only know that people call me a feminist whenever I express sentiments that differentiate me from a doormat or a prostitute." The point: "feminist" was already used as an insult for any woman who refused to be treated as an object.
- **Dictionaries** define feminism as the advocacy of women's rights on the basis of **the equality of the sexes**.
- **Academic writers** stress that feminism is a **perspective**: it puts **gender at the centre** of the analysis of society. A 2025 MCQ asked why feminist perspectives are distinctive; the answer was that **they emphasise the centrality of gender**.

::: simple
A sociologist might study poverty by looking at class. A feminist asks an extra question: **"How are women and men affected differently — and why?"** That extra question, asked about everything, is the feminist perspective.
:::

## 1.3  Where the word came from

- The word began in **France** as *féminisme*. The French women's-rights campaigner **Hubertine Auclert** used "feminist" for herself and her cause in ==1882==, in her journal *La Citoyenne*. (Many books credit the philosopher **Charles Fourier** with inventing the word earlier, but this claim is not firmly proved.)
- The word reached **English in the 1890s**.
- The *ideas* are much older than the word. Writers in many cultures argued for women's education and dignity long before anyone called it "feminism".

::: trap | Feminism did not begin in 1960s America
Many candidates write as if feminism was invented by American women in the 1960s. The **ideas** go back centuries; the **word** is French and dates from the 1880s; organised **movements** began in the 1800s (Seneca Falls, ==1848==). And South Asia had its own early feminist writers (§1.6).
:::

## 1.4  The waves of feminism in brief

Historians often divide Western feminism into **waves**. The metaphor was made popular by the journalist **Martha Weinman Lear** in ==1968==, who wrote of "the second feminist wave". The waves are taught in full in **Topic 3** (feminist movements). Here you need only the outline, because each school belongs mainly to one wave.

::: fig timeline | The four waves of Western feminism, and the schools that belong to each.
@layout vertical
== First wave (c. 1848–1920): votes and legal rights
1792: Wollstonecraft, A Vindication of the Rights of Woman
1848: Seneca Falls Convention, New York
1869: Mill, The Subjection of Women
1920: US women win the vote (19th Amendment)
== Second wave (1960s–1980s): the family, work, body and sexuality
1963: Friedan, The Feminine Mystique
1970: Millett and Firestone — radical feminism
1979: Hartmann — socialist feminism; the domestic-labour debate
== Third wave (1990s–2000s): difference and diversity
1989: Crenshaw names intersectionality
1990: Butler, Gender Trouble — postmodern feminism
1992: Rebecca Walker announces a "Third Wave"
== Fourth wave (c. 2012 onwards): digital activism
2017: #MeToo spreads worldwide
2018: The first Aurat March in Pakistan
:::

::: table | Which schools belong mainly to which wave.
@widths 3,5.2,5.8
Wave | Main demands | Schools most associated with it
First | Vote, property, education, legal equality | **Liberal** feminism; early **Marxist** feminism
Second | Equal pay, reproductive rights, violence, sexuality, housework | **Radical**, **socialist**, **psychoanalytic**, second-wave **liberal**
Third | Diversity among women; identity; culture | **Postmodern**, **Black**, **postcolonial**, **intersectional**
Fourth | Online campaigns against harassment and violence | Draws on all, especially **intersectionality**
:::

::: note
The wave picture is a simplification. Critics point out that it follows **white, Western** history, and that it hides activism between the "waves". Use it as a map, not a law.
:::

## 1.5  The common ground: what all feminists share

The schools disagree about many things, but all accept five basic claims.

1. **Women are disadvantaged** as a group — in power, money, safety, voice and freedom.
2. **This is not natural.** It is produced by society and can be changed.
3. **The personal and the political are linked.** Laws, customs and ideas shape even the most private parts of life.
4. **Women's experience counts as knowledge.** What women know from their lives is valid evidence.
5. **Change is possible and worth working for.**

::: fig venn | What every school shares, and where the schools part company.
@centre Shared: women are disadvantaged; it is made by society; it can be changed
Agreement: the problem exists
Disagreement: its cause, its site and its cure
:::

## 1.6  Feminism in South Asia and the Muslim world

The idea that feminism is purely Western is common in Pakistan. The history shows otherwise.

- **Tarabai Shinde** (Maharashtra) wrote *Stri Purush Tulana* ("A Comparison of Women and Men") in ==1882== — an angry attack on the double standards applied to women and men.
- **Begum Rokeya Sakhawat Hossain** (Bengal) wrote *Sultana's Dream* in ==1905==, a story of a "Ladyland" where men are kept in seclusion and women run a peaceful, scientific country. She also founded a girls' school in Calcutta in ==1911==.
- **Sayyid Mumtaz Ali** of Lahore wrote *Huquq-un-Niswan* ("The Rights of Women", ==1898==), arguing from the Qur'an that Islam gives women rights that Muslim custom had denied.
- In Egypt, **Qasim Amin** published *The Liberation of Women* (==1899==), and **Huda Shaarawi** founded the Egyptian Feminist Union in ==1923==.
- In Pakistan, the **All Pakistan Women's Association (APWA)** was founded by **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan** in ==1949==, and the **Women's Action Forum (WAF)** in ==1981==. (Their history belongs to Topic 3.)

::: pakistan | The Quaid on women's place in the nation
Speaking at Aligarh on ==10 March 1944==, **Quaid-e-Azam Muhammad Ali Jinnah** said: "No nation can rise to the height of glory unless your women are side by side with you." Pakistani feminists of every school quote this line to show that women's equality belongs to the founding vision of the country, not to a foreign agenda.
:::

## 1.7  Myths about feminism

::: table | Common myths about feminism, and the facts.
@widths 5,9
Myth | Fact
"Feminism means hating men." | Every definition above targets **sexism and oppression**, not men. Many feminists are men (Chapter 6).
"Feminism is one ideology." | It is a **family of theories** that disagree sharply with each other (Chapters 2–8).
"Feminism is Western." | South Asian and Muslim writers argued for women's rights in the ==1880s–1900s==, and feminism takes local forms (Chapter 8).
"Feminism is against religion." | Some feminists are secular, but **Muslim feminists** work *from* the Qur'an (Chapter 8).
"Feminism wants women to be like men." | Some schools want **equal treatment**; others value **women's difference** (Chapters 2, 5).
"Women are already equal; feminism is outdated." | Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== on the Global Gender Gap Report ==2026==; female labour-force participation was ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25).
:::

## 1.8  How to classify the schools

There are many ways to sort feminist theories. The syllabus follows the classic list found in textbooks such as **Rosemarie Tong**'s *Feminist Thought* (first edition ==1989==): liberal, radical, Marxist/socialist, psychoanalytic, and postmodern. A simple way to arrange them is along a line from **reform** to **revolution**.

::: fig spectrum | The schools of feminism from "reform the system" to "transform the system".
@left Reform the system
@right Transform the system
10: Liberal — change laws within the system
30: Men's feminism — change men and masculinity
45: Psychoanalytic — change childrearing and the mind
60: Postmodern — unsettle language and categories
78: Socialist and Marxist — change the economy
94: Radical — uproot patriarchy itself
:::

::: eye | Answering "What is feminism?"
Give (1) a simple definition of your own; (2) one quoted definition — Bhasin and Khan for Pakistan, hooks for brevity; (3) the two parts, theory and practice; (4) the common ground of all schools; (5) a sentence on the word's history and the waves; (6) the South Asian roots; and (7) a line showing that the schools disagree on cause and cure. Then move to whatever the question asks next — in 2016, the difference between radical and socialist feminism (Chapter 9).
:::
